From: Sarath Sadasivan Pillai <sarathsp06@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 16:14:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] A stale Co-Review no longer swallows launches, and an update
 leaves agents connected

- Launched again without a backend (none registered since it started, its
  process gone, or its URL not answering), the app restarts itself with that
  launch's arguments (app.relaunch, from the executable on disk, so after an
  update the new version) and quits, so the launch is served instead of logged
  as "launched again" and dropped. Checked at once, as `co-review mcp` is
  waiting, rather than at the next 10-second watch. Restarting beats starting
  the backend again from the main process: Theia starts its backend once, while
  the app starts, and its windows are tied to it.
- The 10-second watch only trusts a server.json written since this app started:
  an older one is a previous run's, whose dead process would have made a fresh
  app quit itself.
- install.sh asks the app to quit first (osascript, bounded to 5 seconds), then
  SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, saying which it took, and fails rather than replace
  the bundle under an app it could not stop. It stops the app's main process
  only: its pattern also matched every agent's `co-review mcp`, which run on the
  app's executable, so an update cut every agent session off Co-Review. Those
  now stay and start the new app on their next call.
